{
  "image_name": "0c1edeccf732b00e320dabafac605b2e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how mitochondria are maintained by a balance of fusion, fission, and selective autophagic removal of damaged organelles. On the left, healthy mitochondria undergo repeated merging and splitting. Fusion is shown as two mitochondria joining through outer-membrane proteins such as MFN1 and the inner-membrane factor OPA1, followed by reorganization of the combined organelle. Fission is shown as a constriction and division event involving DNM1L/DRP1 with adaptor proteins including FIS1 and MFF, producing separate daughter mitochondria.\n\nThe right side shows what happens when a mitochondrion is damaged. It becomes coated with ubiquitin in a pathway labeled PINK1–PARK2-dependent mitophagy. Ubiquitin and mitophagy receptors help recruit a phagophore, which grows around the damaged mitochondrion to form an autophagosome. This structure then matures into an autolysosome after fusion with a lysosome, where the mitochondrial contents are degraded.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a quality-control cycle: normal mitochondria constantly remodel through fusion and fission, while dysfunctional ones are specifically recognized, enclosed, and broken down through mitophagy.",
